-### Setting Your Own Encryption Key
-
-By default, recordings are encrypted with Limitless's server key. You can inject your own
-key to decrypt future recordings:
+- The pendant has its own P-256 key pair and reports the public half in its device info.
+- You make a second P-256 key pair and send the pendant your raw 65-byte public key.
+- Both sides compute ECDH-P256, then HKDF-SHA256 (empty salt and info) to a 128-bit key.
+- Each audio chunk is AES-128-GCM with its own nonce and tag.
 
 ```bash
-# 1. Generate X25519 keypair (saved to keys/ directory)
-pendant crypto keygen
-
-# 2. Send public key to device
-pendant crypto setkey
-
-# 3. Download recordings (auto-decrypts with your private key)
-pendant download
+pendant crypto setkey     # makes your key pair if needed, saves the audio key, sends your public key
+pendant crypto status     # what's set up
+pendant download          # decrypts with your audio key
+pendant crypto decrypt FILE.enc.jsonl   # decrypt chunks saved earlier
 ```
 
-**Important:** Existing recordings encrypted with Limitless's key cannot be decrypted
-without their private key. Only new recordings after `setkey` will use your key.
+Keys live in `~/.config/pendant/keys/`. `audio.key` is all you need to decrypt, so back it up:
+without it, recordings still on the pendant can't be read. If any audio can't be decrypted,
+`download --delete-after` keeps every page on the pendant and saves the chunks whole in a
+`.enc.jsonl` file.
+
+Only recordings made after `setkey` use your key. A factory reset clears it (the pendant then
+records unencrypted). If the Limitless app connects again, it may replace your key with its own.
 
